Warp Perspective 
Explore how to extract and transform a section of an image using pixel coordinates and OpenCV in Python. Understand obtaining corner points, creating a warp perspective matrix, and applying warpPerspective to crop and display image sections effectively.

Sometimes, we might want to work with only a portion of an image. For example, while scanning a document, we only want a part of the document scanned. To perform such tasks, follow the steps mentioned in the illustration.



The lesson map
1.
How can we get only the necessary portions of an image?

Show Answer
Did you find this helpful?
In this lesson, we’ll get a portion of the first button, “Champions League,” from the image below.



The image to be used for warp perspective
Get the pixel points
To get the pixel points of the corners of the image, we can use any image editing tool, such as MS Paint. When we move the cursor to the corner of the area we want to extract, the pixel point will be shown in the bottom left corner of the MS Paint window. This is shown in the following screenshot.



Getting points from MS Paint
We can get the four-pixel points of the “Champions League” button’s corners with this process.

width, height = 400, 100
ptsA = np.float32([[1114, 380], [1726, 380], [1114, 535], [1726, 535]])
ptsB = np.float32([[0, 0], [width, 0], [0, height], [width, height]])
Note: We assign the values of four points of the required portion to four different points to create the image.

Create a warp perspective matrix
To create a warp perspective matrix, we use the getPerspectiveTransform() method of the OpenCV library. We will use the following code to create a warp perspective matrix.

matrix = cv2.getPerspectiveTransform(ptsA, ptsB)
Here, the first parameter is the pixel points of the button’s corners in the original image. The second parameter is the points around the warped image.

Warp the image
To warp the image, we use the warpPerspective() function of the OpenCV library. This function requires three parameters—img, matrix, and (width, height). These represent the image, its warp perspective matrix, and its dimensions, respectively.

warpedImg = cv2.warpPerspective(img, matrix, (width, height))
Display the warped image
To display the warped image, we use the code below:

cv2.imshow("Main",img)
cv2.imshow("WarpedImg",warpedImg)
